WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:01.868
-They love you. We love you.

00:00:01.868 --> 00:00:03.400
Thank you for being here.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.701
I appreciate it, bud.
You look great.

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:08.467
You know, you were very nice
enough -- you were my first

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:11.000
guest ever when I started
"Late Night."

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.534
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:15.067
[ Laughter ]

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:17.968
What we found is
when we had "Late Night" --

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:19.267
we have them for "Tonight Show"
tonight, too.

00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:21.701
But before every show, we have
guests sign a guestbook.

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:22.734
This is real.

00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:25.100
And just say whatever
you want to say,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.767
just sign something to me.

00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:29.501
And so you were the first person
to sign this book.

00:00:29.501 --> 00:00:33.534
And I was just wondering if you
could maybe read what you wrote

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:35.901
'cause this is --
[ Laughter ]

00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:37.534
Can you see it there?

00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:38.801
-[ Clears throat ] Yeah.

00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:41.167
I said, "Jimmy, I think you
really [bleep] up."

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.801
[ Laughter ]

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:45.000
-Appreciate it. Thank you.
Thank you very much.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.534
-You're welcome.
-I appreciate that.

00:00:46.534 --> 00:00:48.100
[ Sad tuba ]

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:52.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.734
-And you know, I gave --
How many years ago was that?

00:00:54.734 --> 00:00:56.501
-That's 13 years ago.
-Wow.

00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:58.534
Because I gave you a dollar,
didn't I?

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:00.767
-Yes, that was "Tonight Show."
Yeah, that was "Tonight Show."

00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:03.000
-So where's the interest
on that?

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:05.868
-No, you don't get any money
back for that.

00:01:05.868 --> 00:01:09.767
A big honor, over the weekend,
the Harry Ransom Center

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.834
at the University of Texas
opened

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:14.234
the De Niro Curator of Film
endowment.

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:15.634
This is a museum...

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:18.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:20.934
...that is everything that
you've saved

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:23.334
throughout your career, right?

00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:26.334
-Well, not everything. A lot.

00:01:26.334 --> 00:01:28.934
Because I see that stuff
would disappear.

00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:34.767
Or I'd be trying on something
in a costume place in L.A.,

00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:39.033
and it'd be something from
another production that you say,

00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:40.934
"Geez, you'd think they'd keep
that or something."

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:45.467
It's kind of a -- It was
a special movie or this or that.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:48.100
And in my own experience,
things would just disappear

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.734
if you didn't hold on to them.

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:53.100
They just would -- People would
take them and so on

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.167
as souvenirs or maybe --
I don't know.

00:01:56.167 --> 00:02:00.601
And even there was a thing
with -- Brando had

00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:06.133
his original "Godfather" script
sold on eBay.

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:11.334
And my assistant pointed this
out to me.

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:15.200
So, it never really -- you know,
it just never had a real place.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.334
It just was wherever,
bought and put away somewhere.

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:23.868
So I thought I'll keep all
this stuff and keep it

00:02:23.868 --> 00:02:27.467
and then one day we started
talking about where it would go

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:31.200
because it was becoming
very costly just to hold it all.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:38.801
And so the Harry Ransom Center
took all my material,

00:02:38.801 --> 00:02:41.267
which I thought was --
I was very happy

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:43.067
because they've got great things
there

00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:45.501
and proud to be part of
that family.

00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:48.501
-That's so cool.
I mean, it's an amazing thing.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:52.467
One of the things I saw that you
gave to them is the --

00:02:52.467 --> 00:02:55.033
You actually got a taxicab
driver's license

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.567
when you did "Taxi Driver."

00:02:57.567 --> 00:03:00.834
You actually were a cabbie
for a little bit.

00:03:00.834 --> 00:03:02.167
That's amazing.

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:05.267
I love the hair there, too.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:06.801
How were you as a cab driver?

00:03:06.801 --> 00:03:08.734
-I was okay.

00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:10.434
I'm a New York driver, you know.

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:12.834
So, I got along. I got around.

00:03:14.534 --> 00:03:16.067
-Did people recognize you?

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:19.534
-Well, there was one guy, I
think, did a double take when he

00:03:19.534 --> 00:03:24.601
looked through the mirror and
wasn't sure whether it was me.

00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:26.501
That was it.
-Yeah, that was it.

00:03:27.934 --> 00:03:33.801
I saw Meryl Streep talked
at the Harry Ransom Center

00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:35.734
in University of Texas.

00:03:35.734 --> 00:03:37.868
And she said some very nice
things about you.

00:03:37.868 --> 00:03:40.834
Said that you were
the inspiration for her being

00:03:40.834 --> 00:03:43.133
the type of actor
that she wanted to be.

00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:46.601
-Well, she said some wonderful,
beautiful things.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:50.367
I was -- I mean, I didn't
even know what to say.

00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:54.467
But it was really great of her,
yeah.

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:57.300
-Not only are you an inspiration
to other actors,

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.934
but also, a lot of people --

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:02.133
other actors do impressions
of you.

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:03.834
I don't know if you've heard.

00:04:03.834 --> 00:04:06.434
[ Laughter ]
-No.

00:04:06.434 --> 00:04:09.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.534 --> 00:04:11.934
-Well, I mean even on this show
in particular, we have --

00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:13.834
this is just some faces --
-No, none of them.

00:04:13.834 --> 00:04:17.300
No, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.267
-Not even close.

00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:21.834
How would you say that you would
do an impression of you?

00:04:21.834 --> 00:04:23.267
-I couldn't. I don't know.

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:25.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:26.834
-That's so funny.

00:04:26.834 --> 00:04:28.834
[ Laughter ]

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:30.067
I don't know how many times
I've done

00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:31.634
an impression of you
in front of you.

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:33.701
You've been very patient.
You've been a very good sport.

00:04:33.701 --> 00:04:35.367
I'm not even going to do it
tonight, I promise.

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:36.601
-Oh, God.

00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:39.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.267
I missed it.

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:43.667
-[ as De Niro ] Let's talk about
your new movie.

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:46.267
[ Laughter ]

00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:48.567
[ Normal voice ]
I do want to talk about
"Amsterdam," by the way.

00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:51.767
This cast is unbelievable --
Christian Bale, Margot Robbie.

00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:54.234
You got Rami Malek,
Michael Shannon.

00:04:54.234 --> 00:04:57.334
It's David O. Russell,
who you've worked with before.

00:04:57.334 --> 00:04:59.634
-Yeah.
-It's a great story.
At the beginning of the movie,

00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:01.767
it says a lot of this
really happened.

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:04.334
And it's actually based on
a story that did happen.

00:05:04.334 --> 00:05:07.667
Is that why you wanted to do
this movie because it's --

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:11.601
-Well, that, the character of
Smedley Butler was based on was

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:14.801
really a great character
who I wasn't aware of that

00:05:14.801 --> 00:05:18.601
David O. Russell told me about
when he was describing

00:05:18.601 --> 00:05:21.033
the whole movie and the story
and so on.

00:05:21.033 --> 00:05:25.467
This went over a couple of --
almost -- a couple of months

00:05:25.467 --> 00:05:28.133
where he would tell me about
this story from time to time

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:30.067
and a possible character
I would do.

00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:33.801
And then he mentioned
this character,

00:05:33.801 --> 00:05:35.701
the one that I played,
Dillenbeck.

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:37.634
And he said it's based on
this guy

00:05:37.634 --> 00:05:40.234
Smedley Butler, this general.

00:05:40.234 --> 00:05:43.667
So he told me to look at
the material on him.

00:05:43.667 --> 00:05:45.400
And the speeches he made,
it was great.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:51.100
It was really -- At that time
especially between the First

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.467
and Second World War,
that period when things

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:57.434
were ramping up with
the fascists in Italy,

00:05:57.434 --> 00:05:59.868
Mussolini and Hitler
and all that.

00:05:59.868 --> 00:06:03.567
I mean, it was exactly
the same thing

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:07.400
then that is now happening.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:11.601
He was a very, very
courageous --

00:06:11.601 --> 00:06:13.934
-And how does David O. Russell
find these stories?

00:06:13.934 --> 00:06:17.501
-Well, that's a good question.
I don't know.

00:06:17.501 --> 00:06:20.834
-It's amazing.
And he does a great job

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:23.434
intertwining and watching it
all together.

00:06:23.434 --> 00:06:25.534
And there's a young actress
in this film --

00:06:25.534 --> 00:06:29.267
Taylor Swift I think is going to
be big one day.

00:06:29.267 --> 00:06:31.100
If you check out this -- Yeah.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.701
You made headlines
over the summer, by the way,

00:06:32.701 --> 00:06:34.634
saying that you have
every Taylor Swift album.

00:06:34.634 --> 00:06:35.601
-Right.

00:06:35.601 --> 00:06:36.934
[ Laughter ]

00:06:36.934 --> 00:06:38.100
-Is that true?

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.100
-No.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.000
[ Laughter, rim shot ]

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.634
I mean, I was kidding, but I --

00:06:43.634 --> 00:06:45.334
but she's wonderful
in the movie.

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:47.334
She really is terrific.
-She's great.

00:06:47.334 --> 00:06:48.434
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:48.434 --> 00:06:49.901
Here's Robert De Niro
in "Amsterdam."

00:06:49.901 --> 00:06:50.868
Take a look at this.

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:52.734
-I'm the doctor.
She's the nurse.

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:53.901
He's the attorney.

00:06:53.901 --> 00:06:55.367
We all met in Belgium.

00:06:55.367 --> 00:06:58.667
Which if you recall is where we
met for the first time.

00:06:58.667 --> 00:07:02.667
-As well as Washington last
summer, at the B.E.F. March.

00:07:02.667 --> 00:07:07.234
-I'll tell you one thing that I
can remember quite clearly.

00:07:07.234 --> 00:07:13.000
You did something --
You sang a song, I believe.

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:15.701
-Which one, sir?

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.067
-Well, that's for you
to remember.

00:07:17.067 --> 00:07:18.834
I-I met thousands of people.

00:07:18.834 --> 00:07:20.734
You just met me twice.

00:07:20.734 --> 00:07:23.133
So you should remember the song.

00:07:24.667 --> 00:07:27.367
I'd like you to sing it now,
then I'll know it's you.

00:07:27.367 --> 00:07:29.934
-Sing a song.
-♪ Did you ever see a dream ♪

00:07:29.934 --> 00:07:32.534
♪ Walking, well, I did ♪

00:07:32.534 --> 00:07:37.100
♪ Did you ever hear a dream
talking, then I did ♪

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.133
-Oh, no, no. That's not it.

00:07:39.133 --> 00:07:41.067
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:07:41.067 --> 00:07:43.400
-That's not it.
No, no, no, not even close.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:49.767
Before you go, I actually have
the "Tonight Show" guestbook.

00:07:49.767 --> 00:07:53.234
I thought maybe if you want,
maybe take another shot

00:07:53.234 --> 00:07:55.334
at writing something.

00:07:55.334 --> 00:07:58.067
-Do I really have to?
-Yeah, go for it.

00:07:58.067 --> 00:08:00.300
I'll date it.
-I don't know what to say.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:01.601
-Think of something.
You can say something.

00:08:01.601 --> 00:08:04.200
Anything you want.
I won't read it.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.267
-Um, okay. Let's see.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:09.701
"You're lucky
you're still here"?

00:08:09.701 --> 00:08:15.467
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:15.467 --> 00:08:17.467
-He is writing that.
He is writing that.

00:08:17.467 --> 00:08:20.300
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.701
That is exactly
what I'm talking about.

00:08:22.701 --> 00:08:24.901
Robert De Niro, everybody.

00:08:24.901 --> 00:08:28.434
"Amsterdam" is in theaters
October 7th.

00:08:28.434 --> 00:08:30.133
We'll be right back
with Chloe Fineman.

00:08:30.133 --> 00:08:31.467
Stick around.

